

# ENCRYPTION_KEY: server-side secret for decryptServerKey() (server.ts) — decrypts
# any provider API keys previously stored server-side in users.json under
# encryptedApiKeys (a legacy path; the live BYOK vault, src/services/
# vaultPassphrase.ts, is fully client-side and doesn't use this). No hardcoded
# fallback: if unset, decryption is simply unavailable (logged once at startup),
# never silently weakened with a guessable default key.
ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Google Sign-In (2026-07-30): no longer needs any client-side config here.
# UserProfileGate.tsx's "Sign in with Google" button goes through Supabase
# Auth's own signInWithOAuth — the Google provider (client ID/secret,
# redirect URLs) is configured entirely in the Supabase dashboard, not via
# an env var read by this app. The old VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID (a client-side
# Google Identity Services client ID) and its in-app settings-gear
# configuration modal have both been removed.

# --- Supabase (Phase 1 hosting migration — docs/planning/hosting-and-topology.md) ---
# VITE_-prefixed so Vite exposes them to client-side code (see src/services/supabaseClient.ts).
# Create a free project at https://supabase.com, then run supabase/schema.sql in its SQL Editor.
# Both values are on the project's Settings > API page. The anon key is safe to expose
# client-side by design (Row Level Security in supabase/schema.sql governs actual access).
VITE_SUPABASE_URL="https://YOUR_PROJECT_REF.supabase.co"
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="YOUR_ANON_KEY"

# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY: server-side/Edge-Function only, NEVER exposed to the
# client (no VITE_ prefix). Used by supabase/functions/* to bypass RLS for admin
# actions (approving registrations/resets) and by scripts/migrate-users-to-supabase.mjs.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="YOUR_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY"

# --- CAPTCHA (Cloudflare Turnstile) — Abuse Protection ---
# VITE_CAPTCHA_SITE_KEY: Public key for Cloudflare Turnstile CAPTCHA on signup form.
# VITE_-prefixed so Vite exposes it to client-side code (safe to expose publicly).
# Create a free Turnstile account at https://dash.cloudflare.com/?to=/:account/turnstile
VITE_CAPTCHA_SITE_KEY="YOUR_CLOUDFLARE_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY"

# CAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY: Server-side secret for verifying CAPTCHA tokens.
# NEVER expose to client. Kept private for server-side verification only.
# Retrieved from Cloudflare dashboard when you create a new site.
CAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY="YOUR_CLOUDFLARE_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY"

# TAVILY_API_KEY: required for the generic authoritative-web-search proxy (search
# vendor decided 2026-07-23 — see docs/planning/target-workflow.md §7 and this file's
# Standing Boundary Rule for the full reasoning and the "basic/advanced search only,
# never Research" constraint). Sign up free at https://tavily.com (no card required),
# then get your key from the dashboard.
# Deliberately NOT VITE_-prefixed, unlike VITE_OPENALEX_API_KEY above: this is a real,
# billable credential (unlike OpenAlex's free rate-limit identifier) and is only ever
# meant to be used by the server-side proxy (per the Standing Boundary Rule), never
# shipped to the client bundle. Same server-only convention as SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
# above.
TAVILY_API_KEY="YOUR_TAVILY_API_KEY"

# --- services/store (new architecture's project persistence) ---
#
# Project persistence needs NOTHING here. It runs over Supabase's HTTPS Data
# API using VITE_SUPABASE_URL + SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (above) — the same
# two values the server already needs to verify tokens. Apply
# supabase/schema_store.sql once, and projects, gates, documents, Rubric
# reports and Claim-Evidence Graphs are durable.
#
# DATABASE_URL is an OPTIONAL fallback for direct SQL (the `pg` driver, via
# src/services/store/pgProjectStore.ts). It is kept because the Data API
# cannot express joins, CTEs, or multi-statement transactions — but it is no
# longer the default, because on a typical PaaS it fails in ways that are
# hard to diagnose. All four of these were hit in production:
#
#   * db.<ref>.supabase.co publishes no A record — it is IPv6-only, and most
#     PaaS egress (Render included) is IPv4-only, so it cannot resolve at all.
#     Use the POOLER host instead: Settings -> Database -> Connection string,
#     "Session pooler" / "Transaction pooler". Never the "Direct" tab.
#   * The pooler needs a tenant-qualified username: postgres.<project-ref>.
#   * Supabase's certificate chains to its own root, so TLS verification fails
#     unless that CA is pinned (already handled in pgProjectStore.ts — do NOT
#     "fix" it with rejectUnauthorized:false).
#   * It needs the database password, which is a separate credential from
#     every other key here, and Supabase's reset dialog shows the new value
#     before it is applied — easy to copy one that never took effect.
#
# See docs/06-decisions/62-DATABASE-ACCESS.md for the full account.
#
# With neither the Data API credentials nor DATABASE_URL set, the server falls
# back to an in-memory store that does NOT survive a restart, and says so
# loudly at startup.
#
# Never commit a real value here — set it as a deployment secret instead.
DATABASE_URL=""

# --- Resend (Email Service) ---
# RESEND_API_KEY: API key from resend.com for sending transactional emails
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL: email address to use as the "from" address for emails
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=""

# --- Scholar AI Engine (Sections 1-18 of the AI Access architecture) ---
#
# USER BYOK credentials vs SCHOLAR PLATFORM credentials — kept structurally
# separate on purpose:
#
#   USER BYOK:     never touches the server. Stored device-only in the
#                   browser's encrypted localStorage (src/services/
#                   userAiSettings.ts). No env var here can affect it —
#                   that is the point (docs/04-architecture/
#                   46-SECURITY-ARCHITECTURE.md §5.3).
#   SCHOLAR PLATFORM (Premium pool): the operator's own provider keys,
#                   funding Premium requests (Section 10). These ARE
#                   stored server-side, deliberately, encrypted with
#                   MASTER_KEY below — see supabase/schema_ai_engine.sql's
#                   header for the full reasoning on why this case differs
#                   from the BYOK one.

# MASTER_KEY: wraps every Premium-pool provider credential
# (public.platform_provider_credentials, via src/services/crypto's
# AES-256-GCM envelope encryption). Base64, 32 bytes decoded — generate with
# `generateMasterKeyBase64()` from src/services/crypto/crypto.ts, or:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Losing this key makes every stored platform credential permanently
# undecryptable — back it up as carefully as any other production secret.
# Never commit a real value here.
MASTER_KEY=""

# TAVILY_API_KEY (see the "Tavily" section above): also powers the AI
# Opportunity Agent's research (server/src/aiEngine/opportunityAgent.ts),
# via the SAME supabase/functions/search-proxy Edge Function every other
# search caller uses — no separate Tavily integration or key.

# MAIN_ADMIN_EMAIL: the hardcoded root admin — server/src/middleware/
# requireAdmin.ts compares this (case-insensitively) against a verified
# token's own email on every request. This account always has every
# permission scope, and is NOT a row in public.admin_users — it can never
# be revoked by anyone (including itself) through the app, since the
# /admins routes only ever edit that table. This is what keeps the admin
# roster from ever being editable into a state with zero admins and no way
# back in short of changing this variable and redeploying. Must be the
# exact email on the Supabase Auth account meant to hold this role — not a
# username, not case-sensitive-mismatched. Leave unset to have no
# hardcoded admin at all (every admin is then an ordinary public.admin_users
# row, granted through the normal flow once at least one exists).
MAIN_ADMIN_EMAIL=""
